# Progress Log

## 2026-09-02 — Authorization flow ergonomics and safety

- Created branch `007-oauth-flow-ergonomics` after onboarding a real second Workspace organization surfaced eleven distinct problems. All are recorded in `specs/007-oauth-flow-ergonomics/notes.md`; the significant ones are summarized here.
- Callback timeout is now `GOOGLE_OAUTH_TIMEOUT` with a 15-minute default. The hardcoded 300 seconds expired three consecutive times during a real sign-in.
- A timeout previously escaped as an unhandled traceback: `run_local_server` raises `WSGITimeoutError`, which subclasses `AttributeError` and is not caught by `main()`. It is now converted to a `RuntimeError` explaining the recovery. This was found only because the user challenged the diagnosis, which prompted reading the library — the first fix written guarded on `credentials is None`, a value the library never returns.
- Under WSL the CLI now registers the Windows browser instead of failing through `xdg-open`'s list of absent Linux browsers. An explicit `BROWSER` setting still wins.
- The authorization URL is now printed with a warning that it and the localhost callback are credential material. A live authorization code was pasted into the AI conversation during this session because a dead callback showed a bare localhost URL with nothing indicating what it was. The code was never redeemed: no token existed, the CLI had exited, nothing was listening on the port, and it cannot be exchanged without the client secret, which never left the machine. No rotation was performed.
- Live outcome: the second organization authorized at 14:38 with exactly one scope (`admin.directory.user.readonly`), a refresh token, and mode `600`. The default profile was unaffected and still reports all three commands ready.
- Documented in the README: the do-not-share warning, `GOOGLE_OAUTH_TIMEOUT`, WSL browser behavior, the truncated-URL `invalid_scope` symptom, the "More than one OAuth client JSON" symptom after adding a profile client, world-readable files copied from Windows, and a full environment-variable reference.
- Open items recorded in notes: `.secrets` file modes are not set automatically; `chrome-debug.bat` is referenced by the README but not tracked in the repository; the Chrome DevTools MCP is configured only in `~/.codex/config.toml`, so it is unavailable to other AI clients despite being presented as a project capability.
- Validation: `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` (28 passed, up from 20), `py_compile`, live WSL browser detection, live `check-setup` for both profiles, and a successful real authorization.

## 2026-09-02 — Per-profile OAuth clients

- Dogfooding 005's `--profile` against a second Workspace organization exposed a blocking gap: profiles owned their tokens but `.secrets` still accepted exactly one OAuth client, and an Internal client only accepts consent from its own organization. Managing two organizations was impossible without downgrading to an External client and weekly reauthorization.
- Created branch `006-per-profile-oauth-clients` and `specs/006-per-profile-oauth-clients/`.
- A named profile now resolves its client to `.secrets/client_secret-<profile>.json`; a missing one fails with the exact expected path before any browser flow starts.
- Added `_is_profile_client` so profile clients are excluded from default-profile discovery. This is the same trap 005 fixed for tokens: without it, adding a second organization's client would trip the "more than one client" error for the default profile.
- `check_setup` accepts a profile, validates that profile's client and token, and names the profile in its header.
- Self-review caught a leak introduced during this work: the readiness line printed the client filename, which embeds the client ID under Google's default naming. Removed; the profile in the header is sufficient to disambiguate, and a test now asserts the filename is absent from output.
- Validation: `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` (20 passed, up from 13), `py_compile`, live `check-setup` for the default profile (still ready, all three commands), and a named-profile check reporting the exact missing client path. No real credentials were read or printed.

## 2026-09-02 — Installer hardening and per-command authorization

- Created branch `005-installer-hardening` and `specs/005-installer-hardening/` from a first-time Ubuntu install report (Python 3.13.7) against the 004 installer.
- Installer: added an `ensurepip` preflight with Debian/Ubuntu remediation naming both `python3-venv` and the version-specific package; removal of an incomplete or failed `.venv` so a failed run is retryable; and an `Installed to: <path>` line in the closing output.
- CLI: added `--profile`, storing each tenant's authorization at `.secrets/token-<profile>.json` while the default profile keeps `.secrets/token.json`. Profile names are validated against a conservative pattern before any filesystem write.
- CLI: OAuth client discovery now excludes every token file via `_is_token_file`, not just the literal `token.json`; a profile token would otherwise have been misread as a second client.
- CLI: replaced the single all-or-nothing `SCOPES` consent with `COMMAND_SCOPES`. Each command requests only its own scope, unioned with scopes the existing token already grants, so consent never drops an earlier grant and a Gmail-only account is no longer blocked by directory scopes. Each command now builds only the API service it uses.
- Readiness gate: `check-setup` reports per-command authorization, passes when at least one command is authorized, and names the missing scope and affected command otherwise. This supersedes 004 FR-005, which is annotated in that spec.
- README: documented `GOOGLE_AI_ADMIN_DIR` and the default install location, warned that an empty directory is not used as the install target, anchored the credential snippet with `cd` into the install directory, documented profiles and per-command scopes, and explained why the OAuth audience choice matters — `admin.directory.*` are restricted scopes, External/Testing refresh tokens expire after seven days, External/Production requires a Google security assessment, and an Internal client in the target Workspace's own Cloud project avoids both.
- README (second pass): added a sample partially-authorized `check-setup` transcript with its exit-code contract, troubleshooting entries for the seven-day External/Testing expiry symptom, missing-scope recovery, and wrong-`--profile` confusion, and a Development section listing every specification plus the test command. The test suite had never been documented.
- Human decision: the reporter's two multi-tenant items were folded into this single spec rather than deferred to 006.
- Validation: `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v` (13 passed, up from 4), `bash -n install.sh`, `py_compile`, CLI `--help`, live `check-setup` (ready, all three commands), unknown-profile and unsafe-profile runs (exit 2, no files written), and three simulated installer runs — missing `ensurepip` (guided failure, no directory changes), poisoned `.venv` (removed, rebuilt, dependencies installed), and fresh install from an empty directory into `GOOGLE_AI_ADMIN_DIR` (empty directory untouched, path reported).
- Merged to `main` as PR #4. Post-merge validation on `main`: unittest suite (13 passed) and live `check-setup` (ready, all three commands); the published `install.sh` on `main` is byte-identical to the merged file. Spec 004 had already merged as PR #3, so 005 targeted `main` directly rather than being stacked.
- Still not validated: the guided `ensurepip` failure on a real Debian/Ubuntu machine without `python3-venv`; it has only been simulated with a stub interpreter.

## 2026-09-02 — User-friendly Workspace bootstrap

- Created branch `004-user-friendly-bootstrap` and specification for a one-command installer and local Google readiness gate.
- Added `install.sh` for existing-checkout or fresh `~/.google-ai-admin` setup, isolated Python dependencies, and safe post-install diagnostics.
- Added `check-setup` to validate dependencies, private `.secrets` permissions, Desktop OAuth client structure, token privacy, and every required OAuth scope without contacting Google or printing secret values.
- Reframed README, startup guidance, constitution, and secrets guidance around AI-assisted Google Workspace work, least privilege, and human confirmation for mutations.
- Added fixture-based setup tests and shell validation.
- Validation: `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v` (4 passed), `bash -n install.sh`, Python compilation, CLI help, and live local `check-setup` (ready).

## 2026-09-02 — Monthly dependency audit

